      "body": "## Finding description and impact\n`GTELaunchpadV2PairFactory.createPair` uses a different CREATE2 salt (including `launchpadLp` and `launchpadFeeDistributor`) when CREATE2ing a pair. The `pairFor()` function (used by `Launchpad` to calculate the pair address) still uses the old salt (just `keccak256(token0, token1)`). This asynchronous behavior causes the pair address calculated by `Launchpad` to differ from the actual pair address created by the Factory, causing transactions referring to the wrong address to fail or assign tokens to an address that does not contain the code (tokens lost).\n\nProblematic parts:\n1.\tIn `GTELaunchpadV2PairFactory.createPair()`.\n```solidity\nfunction createPair(address tokenA, address tokenB) external returns (address pair) {\n    // ...\n    (address _launchpadLp, address _launchpadFeeDistributor) =\n        msg.sender == launchpad ? (launchpadLp, launchpadFeeDistributor) : (address(0), address(0));\n    \n    bytes32 salt = keccak256(abi.encodePacked(token0, token1, _launchpadLp, _launchpadFeeDistributor)); // ❌ NEW SALT\n    // ...\n}\n```\nThe factory passes `_launchpadLp` and `_launchpadFeeDistributor` to the salt.\n\n2. The `pairFor()` function in `Launchpad` calculates the pair address using the old salt.\n```solidity\nfunction pairFor(address factory, address tokenA, address tokenB) internal view returns (IUniswapV2Pair pair) {\n    (address token0, address token1) = sortTokens(tokenA, tokenB);\n    pair = IUniswapV2Pair(\n        address(\n            uint160(\n                uint256(\n                    keccak256(\n                        abi.encodePacked(\n                            hex\"ff\",\n                            factory,\n                            keccak256(abi.encodePacked(token0, token1)), // ❌ OLD SALT\n                            uniV2InitCodeHash\n                        )\n                    )\n                )\n            )\n        )\n    );\n}\n```\n`pairFor` doesn't include launchpad params, resulting in a different address.\n\n`Launchpad` expects the pair to be at the `pairFor()` address (calculated), but Factory deploys the pair to the address based on the salt that looks at the extra params (actual). This mismatch breaks the deterministic assumption.\n\n## Impact\nLaunchpad can send tokens (e.g., bonding seed tokens, quote tokens) to a predicted address (`pairFor`), but no contract exists at that address, causing the tokens to become stuck. Functions attempting to call a pair (e.g., `pair.skim()`) on a predicted address will revert (`extcodesize == 0`). Launchpad reward flows (e.g., `endRewardsAccrual`) or bootstrapping liquidity (addLiquidity) will break, causing the graduation/market-making process to fail.\n\n## Recommendation \nUpdate salt format di `Launchpad`.\n```diff\nfunction pairFor(address factory, address tokenA, address tokenB) internal view returns (IUniswapV2Pair pair) {\n    // Sort token addresses according to Uniswap standards\n    (address token0, address token1) = sortTokens(tokenA, tokenB);\n   \n+   // Define launchpad parameters based on context\n+   // If called for a token launched via launchpad, use the launchpad parameter\n+   address _launchpadLp = address(0);\n+   address _launchpadFeeDistributor = address(0);\n   \n+   // Check if this is a launch token by looking at the launch data\n+   if (_launches[tokenA].quote != address(0) || _launches[tokenB].quote != address(0)) {\n+       _launchpadLp = address(launchpadLPVault);\n+       _launchpadFeeDistributor = address(distributor);\n+   }\n   \n    // Calculate the pair address using the CREATE2 formula\n    pair = IUniswapV2Pair(\n        address(\n            uint160(\n                uint256(\n                    keccak256(\n                        abi.encodePacked(\n                            hex\"ff\",                                    \n                            factory,                                   \n-                           keccak256(abi.encodePacked(token0, token1)), // Salt (OLD: only token addresses)\n+                           keccak256(abi.encodePacked(token0, token1, _launchpadLp, _launchpadFeeDistributor)), // Salt (NEW: include launchpad params)\n                            uniV2InitCodeHash                          \n                        )\n                    )\n                )\n            )\n        )\n    );\n}\n```",
      "proofOfConcept": "## Scenario Considerations\n1. Launchpad calculates the pair address (using pairFor with an OLD salt)\n- Launchpad calls the internal `pairFor(factory, tokenA, tokenB)` to calculate the pair address `A` (using `keccak256(token0, token1)`).\n2. Launchpad sends the token or calls the pair to the calculated address (`A`)\n- Example: `transfer(quote, A, amount)` or calling `pairFor().skim(owner())`.\n3. The factory actually deploys the pair to a different address (`B`)\n- The factory uses a salt that includes `launchpadLp` and `launchpadFeeDistributor`, so CREATE2 deploys the pair to address `B != A`.\n4. Address `A` does not contain a contract (`extcodesize == 0`)\n- When Launchpad calls `A`, the call reverts/fails.\n- If Launchpad (or user) sends token to A because it trusts deterministic address, token will be in EOA-like address without code → token stuck.\n\n## POC\nAdd to `PoCLaunchpad.t.sol`.\n```solidity\nimport {ILaunchpad} from \"contracts/launchpad/interfaces/ILaunchpad.sol\";\nimport {IUniswapV2Pair} from \"@gte-univ2-core/interfaces/IUniswapV2Pair.sol\";\nimport {IUniswapV2FactoryMinimal} from \"contracts/launchpad/interfaces/IUniswapV2FactoryMinimal.sol\";\n\nfunction test_CREATE2AddressMismatch_CausesIncorrectPairCalculation() external {\n    // Step 1: Launch a new token\n    address launchedToken = launchpad.launch{value: 0}(\"TestToken\", \"TEST\", \"https://test.com\");\n    address quoteTokenAddr = address(launchpad.currentQuoteAsset());\n        \n    // Step 2: Get factory and calculate pair address using Launchpad's logic\n    IUniswapV2FactoryMinimal factoryContract = IUniswapV2FactoryMinimal(launchpad.uniV2Router().factory());\n    address calculatedPairAddr = _replicateLaunchpadPairForLogic(\n        address(factoryContract), \n        launchedToken, \n        quoteTokenAddr\n    );\n        \n    // Step 3: Trigger graduation to force actual pair creation\n    _triggerGraduation(launchedToken);\n        \n    // Step 4: Get the actual deployed pair address\n    address actualPairAddr = factoryContract.getPair(launchedToken, quoteTokenAddr);\n        \n    // Step 5: Demonstrate the issue\n    _demonstrateVulnerability(calculatedPairAddr, actualPairAddr);\n}\n\nfunction _replicateLaunchpadPairForLogic(\n    address factory, \n    address tokenA, \n    address tokenB\n) internal view returns (address pair) {\n    // Sort tokens exactly like Launchpad's sortTokens function\n    (address token0, address token1) = tokenA < tokenB ? (tokenA, tokenB) : (tokenB, tokenA);\n    require(token0 != address(0), \"ZERO_ADDRESS\");\n    require(tokenA != tokenB, \"IDENTICAL_ADDRESSES\");\n        \n    // Get init code hash from launchpad\n    bytes memory initCodeHash = launchpad.uniV2InitCodeHash();\n        \n    // Calculate pair address using OLD salt format (what Launchpad uses)\n    // This is INCORRECT because it doesn't include launchpadLp and launchpadFeeDistributor\n    pair = address(\n        uint160(\n            uint256(\n                keccak256(\n                    abi.encodePacked(\n                        hex\"ff\",                                    // CREATE2 prefix\n                        factory,                                   // Factory address\n                        keccak256(abi.encodePacked(token0, token1)), // OLD salt format\n                        initCodeHash                               // Init code hash\n                    )\n                )\n            )\n        )\n    );\n}\n\nfunction _triggerGraduation(address launchedToken) internal {\n    uint256 bondingSupply = launchpad.BONDING_SUPPLY();\n    uint256 quoteNeeded = launchpad.quoteQuoteForBase(launchedToken, bondingSupply, true);\n        \n    // Setup for buying\n    quoteToken.approve(address(launchpad), type(uint256).max);\n    quoteToken.mint(address(this), quoteNeeded);\n        \n    // Buy all bonding supply to trigger graduation\n    launchpad.buy(ILaunchpad.BuyData({\n        token: launchedToken,\n        account: address(this),\n        recipient: address(this),\n        amountOutBase: bondingSupply,\n        maxAmountInQuote: quoteNeeded\n    }));\n}\n\nfunction _demonstrateVulnerability(address calculatedAddr, address actualAddr) internal {\n    // PROOF 1: Addresses are different\n    assertNotEq(calculatedAddr, actualAddr, \"Calculated != Actual pair address\");\n        \n    // PROOF 2: Calculated address has no contract deployed\n    uint256 calculatedCodeSize;\n    assembly { calculatedCodeSize := extcodesize(calculatedAddr) }\n    assertEq(calculatedCodeSize, 0, \"Calculated address has no deployed contract\");\n        \n    // PROOF 3: Actual address has deployed contract\n    uint256 actualCodeSize;\n    assembly { actualCodeSize := extcodesize(actualAddr) }\n    assertGt(actualCodeSize, 0, \"Actual address should have deployed contract\");\n        \n    // PROOF 4: Operations on calculated address fail (demonstrated by code size check)\n    // We avoid the problematic vm.expectRevert() by just checking code size\n    assertTrue(calculatedCodeSize == 0, \"Calculated address is not a contract - calls would fail\");\n        \n    // PROOF 5: Operations on actual address work\n    IUniswapV2Pair(actualAddr).skim(address(this)); // This should work\n        \n    // PROOF 6: Demonstrate potential fund loss scenario\n    _demonstrateFundLossScenario(calculatedAddr);\n        \n    // Log evidence\n    emit log_named_address(\"Calculated pair address (WRONG)\", calculatedAddr);\n    emit log_named_address(\"Actual pair address (CORRECT)\", actualAddr);\n    emit log_named_uint(\"Calculated address code size\", calculatedCodeSize);\n    emit log_named_uint(\"Actual pair address code size\", actualCodeSize);\n}\n\nfunction _demonstrateFundLossScenario(address calculatedAddr) internal {\n    // Scenario: If someone sends tokens to the calculated address thinking it's the pair\n    // Those tokens would be permanently lost because no contract exists there\n        \n    // Mint some tokens to demonstrate\n    uint256 testAmount = 1000 ether;\n    quoteToken.mint(address(this), testAmount);\n        \n    // Show that tokens can be sent to calculated address but are lost\n    quoteToken.transfer(calculatedAddr, testAmount);\n        \n    // Verify tokens are at calculated address but inaccessible\n    uint256 balanceAtCalculated = quoteToken.balanceOf(calculatedAddr);\n    assertEq(balanceAtCalculated, testAmount, \"Tokens sent to calculated address\");\n        \n    // Show that these tokens cannot be recovered (no contract to interact with)\n    uint256 codeSize;\n    assembly { codeSize := extcodesize(calculatedAddr) }\n    assertEq(codeSize, 0, \"No contract at calculated address - tokens are lost!\");\n        \n    emit log_named_uint(\"Tokens lost at calculated address\", balanceAtCalculated);\n}\n```",
